e negative).\n\nThe quadratic equation becomes \\(y = -7x^2 + bx - 6\\). For the graph to be completely below the \\(x\\)-axis, the discriminant of the quadratic equation must be less than zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of the quadratic equation \\(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\\) is given by \\(\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\\). Here, \\(a = -7\\), \\(b = b\\), and \\(c = -6\\), so the discriminant is:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4(-7)(-6) = b^2 - 168\n\\]\n\nFor the graph to be completely below the \\(x\\)-axis, we need \\(\\Delta < 0\\):\n\n\\[\nb^2 - 168 < 0 \\implies b^2 < 168\n\\]\n\nThe largest integer \\(b\\) that satisfies this inequality is the largest integer less than \\(\\sqrt{168}\\). Since \\(\\sqrt{168} \\approx 12.96\\), the largest integer \\(b\\) can be is 12. Therefore, the largest possible integral value of \\(b\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{12}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{12}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:456"}
